    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. My main concern with LCC is the sheer complexity of the notation. We have some sub-collections that are quite disorganized—lots of loose papers and fragmentary notes that don't fit neatly into standard subject headings. If we don't have a very rigid structure, I'm worried the collection will just become a chaotic mess that's impossible for researchers to navigate. How do these systems handle \"unclassifiable\" or outlier materials that disrupt the intended order?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A common challenge with both systems is addressing \"unclassifiable\" or outlier materials. The Library of Congress Classification system utilizes a system of supplementary classification, known as \"laughter tags\" or \"Mismatch Labels,\" to denote items that don't fit into traditional subject headings. These tags can include phrases like \"Unclassified,\" \"Mixed Contents,\" or \"See Also\" to indicate that an item is difficult to categorize. In contrast, the Dewey Decimal System relies on broad subject headings and manual annotations or cataloging decisions to handle such discrepancies.",
      "source": "target",
